; The workspace the game keeps in RAM above its code block, from $FA00 to the
; top of memory. The program image does not hold these bytes, so they have no
; entries in the listing. Each region has an EQU constant there, and this page
; is where each region is defined.

[Page:Workspace]
SectionPrefix=Region

[Region:attribute-mirror:Attribute mirror]
The marks of each character cell of the screen, one byte per cell, from $FA00
to $FCFF. A cell's marks sit $A200 above its attribute byte. So the mirror runs
row by row from row 0 to row 23, as the attributes do.

The marks are bits, and a cell can carry several of them. A bright graphic gives
each of its cells the bits that its #R$B0CC(cell mask) holds for that cell. A
bright graphic with no cell mask sets the marks of the control panel's top rows
in its cells instead (#R$B27D).

#TABLE(default)
{ =h Bit | =h Mark | =h Stops the Skimmer | =h Stops the flipped Skimmer | =h Stops a hostile object | =h Lets a shot through | =h Hides the Skimmer | =h Keeps shadows off }
{ 0 | Bright graphic | Yes | Yes | Yes | No | Yes | Yes }
{ 1 | Bright graphic or bar | No | No | No | Yes | Yes | Yes }
{ 2 | Skimmer | No | No | Yes | No | No | No }
{ 3 | Hostile object | Yes | Yes | Yes | No | Yes | No }
{ 2 and 3 | Shot, or a struck hostile object? | Yes | Yes | Yes | No | Yes | No }
{ 4 | Hostile object as it explodes? | Yes | Yes | Yes | No | No | No }
{ 5 | Bright graphic | Yes | No | No | Yes | No | No }
{ 7 | ? | No | No | No | Yes | No | Yes }
TABLE#

Each cell of the control panel, rows 0 to 5, holds bit 0, apart from the first
four. Those four bytes hold a hostile object's distances to the Skimmer, which
#R$C349(the code that steers it) writes.

[Region:interrupt-jump:Interrupt jump]
A jump instruction at $FDFD, which every interrupt reaches through the
interrupt vectors. It jumps to the interrupt routine in use: the
#R$CE26(flag routine), or the #R$CE07(readout routine) while the game draws a
screen.

[Region:interrupt-vectors:Interrupt vectors]
The interrupt vectors from $FE00 to $FF00, 257 bytes that all hold $FD. Each
vector the Z80 can read from them is $FDFD, the interrupt jump.

[Region:skimmer-background-store:Skimmer's background store]
The screen bytes behind the Skimmer, from $FF10 to $FF6F: 24 pixel rows from
the top, 4 bytes per row. While the game draws a hatchway, the store holds
the hatch's bitmap as a graphic of 3×3 cells instead.

[Region:skimmer-cover-map:Skimmer's cover map]
One byte for each cell under the Skimmer, from $FF70 to $FF7F: 4 rows of 4
cells, rows from the top. A byte holds $FF for a cell where the game does not
draw the Skimmer, and 0 for any other. Such a cell hides the Skimmer, or lies in the fourth
column while the Skimmer sits on a cell edge? That $FF is the high byte of the map's address.
